Web-parity mapping (read source end-to-end):
  - targetRef + browser Fullscreen API  -> @Binding<Bool> isFullscreen; host owns the
       presentation (.fullScreenCover on iOS / window toggle on macOS). Esc/swipe flips the
       binding == the web fullscreenchange listener keeping the icon honest.
  - document.fullscreenEnabled / testHookSupported -> `supported` flag (default true on Apple;
       fullScreenCover/window toggle always available). Unsupported -> EmptyView (web return null).
  - Lucide Maximize/Minimize -> SF Symbols arrow.up.left.and.arrow.down.right /
       arrow.down.right.and.arrow.up.left, swapped with .contentTransition(.symbolEffect(.replace)).
  - aria-label/title/aria-pressed flip together -> accessibilityLabel (Enter/Exit) + .help tooltip
       + .isSelected trait; identifier "fullscreen-button" (web data-testid).
  - ariaLabelEnter/ariaLabelExit -> optional label overrides (default from catalog).
  - size 'sm'|'md'|'lg' -> FullscreenButtonSize -> shared TSButton(variant:.ghost,size:).
  - i18n: every string via FullscreenButtonStrings (P1/S10); no English literals in code.
  - Data sources: web hook = useTranslation only (no data fetch). Like ToolCard, this is a pure
       presentational primitive that owns no data, so loading/empty/error/stale/offline belong to
       the host content it expands, not the button. No direct HTTP from the view (acceptance met).

